{"number": 65, "text": "                         One      house,       two      houses\n           2    3)       Syllables\n                        Listen to these three lists of words. The words in list 1 have three parts — we say they have three\n                        syllables. The words in list 2 have two syllables, and the words in list 3 have one syllable.\n                         1                  2                   3\n                         in-ter-net         Six-ty              six\n                         un-der-line        un-der              line\n                         un-der-lined       u-nit               lines\n                        al-pha-bet          hou-ses             house\n                        con-so-nant         go-ing              goes\n                        Some  words  have more  than three syllables: television has four syllables, for example, and\n                        geographical has five syllables. Listen.\n                        te-le-vi-sion\n                        ge-o-gra-phi-cal\n                        The  simplest type of syllable is just a vowel sound,\n                        like /u:/. People often say the vowel sound /u:/\n                        (usually written Ooh) when  they are pleased or\n                        surprised. Listen.\n                        Ooh,  that’s nice!\n                        Ooh,  thank you  very much!\n\n\n                                                                                                Ooh, thank you very much!\n                        Some  syllables have one or more consonant sounds  before the vowel. Listen.\n                        /s/ + fuc/ = /su:/ This is the name Sue.\n                            +     =       blue\n                        Some  syllables have one or more consonants after the vowel. Listen.\n                           +    =     eat\n                           +     =      east\n                        Some  syllables have consonants before and after the vowel. Listen.\n                            +     + /2/ =       news\n                           +    +      =        fields\n                             +    + A/ = /stri:t/ street\n                        Usually, the number of syllables in a word is the number of vowel sounds — not the number of\n                        vowel letters. Listen.\n                        worked         (2 vowel letters but only 1 vowel sound        so only 1 syllable)\n                        different      (3 vowel letters but only 2 vowel sounds /‘difrant/, so only 2 syllables)\n                        interesting    (4 vowel letters but only 3 vowel sounds            so only 3 syllables)\n                        Sometimes  the sound    can be a syllable with no vowel sound. Listen.\n                        botde                  (2 syllables    +\n                        syllable               (3 syllables /'si/ + /la/ +\n                            be ready soon.     (2 syllables /it/ +\n\n\n              64           English Pronunciation in Use (Elementary)", "char_count": 3016, "mean_conf": 85.93, "word_count": 326, "words": [{"text": "One", "x0": 451, "y0": 277, "x1": 586, "y1": 339, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "house,", "x0": 618, "y0": 277, "x1": 835, "y1": 347, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "two", "x0": 864, "y0": 282, "x1": 992, "y1": 340, "conf": 96, "bold": true}, {"text": "houses", "x0": 1024, "y0": 278, "x1": 1256, "y1": 340, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "2", "x0": 208, "y0": 295, "x1": 269, "y1": 393, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "3)", "x0": 298, "y0": 291, "x1": 371, "y1": 445, "conf": 63, "bold": false}, {"text": "Syllables", "x0": 452, "y0": 363, "x1": 751, "y1": 441, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "@a)", "x0": 353, "y0": 482, "x1": 426, "y1": 555, "conf": 41, "bold": false}, {"text": "Listen", "x0": 444, "y0": 491, "x1": 550, "y1": 546, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "to", "x0": 567, "y0": 491, "x